      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the breakdown. so when you say plaque builds up, is that like it's physically blocking the blood or does it make the arteries narrower? i'm just trying to visualize how it actually causes a problem over time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, exactly. The plaque is made of cholesterol, fats, and other substances, and it can physically block the arteries. This reduces blood flow to the heart and other parts of the body. When plaque becomes too thick, it can also narrow the arteries, making it harder for enough blood to flow through. Over time, this can lead to things like chest pain (angina), shortness of breath, and heart attacks. It's like trying to push a thin, soft lump through a straw – eventually, you'll have a difficult time, and that illustrates how plaque can prevent normal blood flow.",
